# Line ending policy
#
# Background: this repository previously had no .gitattributes at all, so line
# endings depended entirely on each developer's core.autocrlf. On Windows,
# autocrlf=true writes checked-out files as CRLF, while Prettier (endOfLine
# defaults to "lf"), Unix scripts, and CI (ubuntu-latest) all work with LF.
# The same code therefore ended up with different line endings per machine:
#   - `npm run format:check` failed wholesale on Windows (the content was in
#     fact fully compliant);
#   - line-ending noise leaked into diffs, making real changes hard to spot.
#
# Policy: LF everywhere, both in the repository and in the working tree
# (`eol=lf` constrains the blob and the checkout alike). Only Windows
# command-line native formats use CRLF.
#
# Why LF rather than CRLF:
#   1. Every blob already in Git is LF (including .sh / .bat). Switching to
#      CRLF would rewrite the entire repository; choosing LF costs zero blob
#      changes and merely brings the working tree back in line.
#   2. *.sh must be LF -- a CRLF shebang makes Linux/macOS fail with
#      "bad interpreter". `eol=lf` prevents this by construction instead of by
#      convention.
#   3. CI (frontend build/tests) runs on ubuntu-latest, so LF matches CI.
#
# Note: the eol setting in `.gitattributes` takes precedence over
# core.autocrlf, so Windows users do not need to change their global config
# for this file to take effect.

# Default: detect text files automatically and normalize them to LF
* text=auto eol=lf

# -- CRLF exceptions --------------------------------------------------------
# Windows batch files are traditionally CRLF. Pure LF is not parsed entirely
# consistently across cmd.exe versions (especially for edge syntax such as
# goto/call and parenthesized blocks), and .bat files only ever run on Windows
# and never enter the Linux/CI flow -- so following the platform convention and
# using CRLF is the safest choice.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# -- Explicitly declared as text (avoid binary heuristic misdetection) ------
# These extensions all contain UTF-8 text, but an individual file could be
# misdetected as binary by text=auto (e.g. due to a missing newline or high-bit
# characters), which would make it silently skip line-ending normalization.
*.ts    text eol=lf
*.tsx   text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.cjs   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.rs    text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.ps1   text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf

# -- Binary: no line-ending conversion whatsoever ---------------------------
# Marking these explicitly keeps Git from mistaking binary content for text and
# corrupting it.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.icns binary
*.webp binary
*.svg  text eol=lf
*.onnx binary
*.pt   binary
*.bin  binary
*.dll  binary
*.so   binary
*.dylib binary
*.a    binary
*.lib  binary
*.exe  binary
*.zip  binary
*.7z   binary
*.gz   binary
*.wav  binary
*.mp3  binary
*.flac binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
